'''''Nicotiana alata''''' is a species of [[Nicotiana|tobacco]]. It is called '''jasmine tobacco''',<ref>{{PLANTS|id=NIAL|taxon=Nicotiana alata|access-date=29 January 2016}}</ref> '''sweet tobacco''',<ref name=BSBI07>{{BSBI 2007 |access-date=2014-10-17 }}</ref> '''winged tobacco''', '''''tanbaku''''', and  '''Persian tobacco'''.

''Nictoiana alata'' is mainly grown as an [[ornamental plant]]; numerous [[cultivar]]s and [[hybrid (biology)|hybrid]]s are derived from it. In [[Iran]], [[narghila]] tobacco is sometimes produced from ''N. alata''; it is not chopped like cigarette tobacco, but broken up by hand.

==Description==

The flowering period of ''Nicotina alata'' in the northern hemisphere ranges from summer through fall. 
The flowers of common varieties open during the late afternoon or evening and release a distinct, floral scent throughout the night. 
The commonly cultivated varieties may have flowers in white, yellow, pink, crimson, lime green or maroon red.

Pollination of plants in the genus ''Nicotina'' is mainly carried out by nocurnal species of moths.<ref>{{cite press release|author1=Danny Kessler|author2= Celia Diezel|author3= Ian T Baldwin|title=Changing pollinators as a means of escaping herbivores|url= https://www.mpg.de/616998/pressRelease20100114|access-date=July 23, 2026}}</ref>

The plant usually grows 30 to 60&nbsp;cm tall, individual plants should be spaced apart by 30 to 40&nbsp;cm. They grow best in full sun to partial shade, in moist soil, given that ''Nicotina alata'' is not drought-tolerant.

==Cultivation==
Seedlings do best when gradually introduced to outdoor environment over the course of a week. Plants tend to self-sow. (Color may be the original or occasionally several colors on one plant).
[[Deadheading (flowers)|Deadhead]] spent flower stalks for best blooming results.

